  "Number": 29,
  "Question": "Which of the following sets of \\(x\\)-values satisfy the inequality \\(2x^2+x\\lt6?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\lt x\\lt\\dfrac32\\)",
    "B": "\\(x\\gt\\dfrac32\\) or \\(x\\lt-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x\\lt\\dfrac32\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac32\\lt x\\lt2\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\lt-2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Moving everything to one side lets us determine when a product is negative:\n\\[\n2x^2+x-6=(2x-3)(x+2)<0.\n\\]\nThe corresponding boundary equation has roots \\(-2\\) and \\(\\frac32\\). A product is negative precisely when its factors have opposite signs.\n\nIf \\(x<-2\\), both factors are negative, so their product is positive. If \\(-2<x<\\frac32\\), then \\(2x-3<0\\) and \\(x+2>0\\), so the product is negative. If \\(x>\\frac32\\), both factors are positive.\n\nAt either boundary the product is zero, which does not satisfy the strict inequality. Therefore the complete solution is \\(\\boxed{-2<x<\\frac32}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
